so far during this pandemic whenever the  government has introduced new  restrictions  the labour party has supported them but  will that continue well labour's  shadow business secretary lucy powell  joins us now from manchester thank you  uh for being with us uh lucy powell so  the prime minister is expected to  announce tomorrow that families will be  able to meet up over christmas  is that a good idea is that a good idea  well let's see what those uh plans are  but i think what we now need  for the first time really in this uh  pandemic  is we need a lot more clarity we need a  blueprint  a route map to take us through from now  through to to next year so that families  know uh  what they're supposed to be doing when  more importantly or just as importantly  so that businesses  can plan whether they are reopening and  if they are  under what terms and and how they can  continue over the coming weeks and there  are still  many many businesses who despite the  briefings in the papers today  won't know whether they can now stock up  on food whether they can start taking  bookings again  whether they can plan to reopen their  businesses so  we need methodical measured  uh clarity not what we've seen before  which is  over briefing then pulling back the  drawbridge at the last minute  mixed messages stay at home go to work  stay at home go to work  we need this now to be a route map  through to next year when hopefully  the vaccine and mass testing will will  come on stream and make things easier  for all of us  so you say on a route map but that's  what we're going to get tomorrow isn't  it we're going to  find out what the plan is for christmas  we're going to find out what the tier  system is going to be that's going to  replace  the national lockdown in england and  then on thursday we'll find out  after the data comes in who's going into  what tier and the question was about  christmas uh public health england has  said  that for every day that restrictions are  relaxed over christmas we'll have to pay  for it with five days of increased  restrictions so what's labour's position  should restrictions be relaxed over  christmas  well look i think we all want to be able  to see our families in some way  over christmas but we don't want to do  that in a way that we then have to pay  a heavy price in in the following weeks  i think what we want is  is measured uh clarity uh so that we can  yes see our families in some small way i  think we all recognize that christmas is  going to be a very very different kind  of christmas  uh this year but it's not just about  christmas it is about  the coming weeks and the weeks after  christmas as well and that's why we need  this clear  route map instead of you know what we  get from the government is just sort of  focusing on  one small aspect which then gets over  briefed and overplayed  and then they have to pull back the  drawbridge at the last minute and you  say  we'll find out all these things this  week i don't know whether we will  and what criteria the government is  going to be  uh using for that because if you're a  hairdresser's in harlow this morning or  a  restaurant in manchester or or a bar in  newcastle  or a brewery in middlesbrough you don't  know whether you can start to restock  whether you can start to brew the beer  whether you can start to take the  bookings  and if you're in the events industry or  the weddings industry or aviation  you will your businesses be able to  begin  to start working and operating again and  these are businesses that are now  absolutely on their knees after many  many many months of  real difficulty of mixed messages of  changing  uh guidelines of changing uh measures  and and really now you know this whole  point of this national lockdown  was to have a reset moment if you like  uh and i worry that we've lost  two weeks of that now with with the  psycho dramas going on in number 10  and the infighting in government they  could have used this time much better  to make it really clear to people what  are going to be the criteria to go into  what's here  what measures will be in those tiers and  how we can all have a route map  from here through to next year okay i'm  still not  totally clear i'll be honest what  labour's position is on  christmas but we should move on to the  spending review of course  which we're expecting will be dominated  by the pretty dire state of the public  finances some really difficult decisions  for the chancellor to make we're  expecting him to potentially announce a  freeze on public sector pay  given the state of the finances given  the fact that uk debt is now bigger than  gdp  these kind of measures something that  the chancellor needs to consider  well i don't think he does need to  consider that  at this point in time because actually  at this point in time when we're in the  middle of a crisis we're not even  vaguely in the recovery phase yet when  you're in the middle of a crisis  you can't cut your way out of that  crisis and you can't really  do that when you're at the beginnings of  the recovery either because you'll choke  off the recovery  in people stopping going out and  spending  their money and helping getting the  economy going so yeah we've got what  we've got to remember about the context  of this comprehensive spending review  is that we are in a much worse position  than many of our other  counterparts in terms of countries and  economies  we've seen the biggest drop in gdp of  all of our european competitors  and we're in a much worse position  because the government's uh constant  sort of  playing catch up and coming to the to  the table too late with support packages  and measures and frankly a lot of what  they're bringing forward doesn't meet  the scale  of the challenge that we face so we're  in a worse position  and economically it would be the wrong  thing to do to cut  back the public finances just at this  point in time  but of course it would be morally  absolutely a kick in the teeth wouldn't  it  for all those front-line workers who've  helped support us  through this pandemic from the bin uh  collectors to those working  in public health in the much in the  mortuaries the fire fighters and so on  this would be an absolute kick in the  teeth for them  after what has been a horrendous few  months and will still be a difficult few  months to come  well let's um we'll put some of that to  the chancellor rishi sunak  when he's on the program later on this  morning we're getting  to crunch point finally should we say on  brexit now of course i know that there's  still some key decisions to be  ironed out particularly on fishing and  level playing field but at the same time  we know  we know really the direction at least of  where this trade deal is going and it's  going to come down to  being a choice between quite a basic  free trade deal  or no deal so what is labour's  position are you inclined to support the  trade deal  when it comes back to the house of  commons  well we've always said that we want  there to be a deal  that is our starting point we have now  left the eu so the arguments about leave  and remain  are over we're now coming to the end  we're very near the end  of the transition arrangement and the  very  best uh situation that we could get for  this country in that context  is a trade deal is a deal with the with  the eu  yeah we would like that to be as  comprehensive a deal as possible so that  businesses and jobs can be protected in  this deeply  uncertain time the last thing businesses  need right now is all the uncertainty so  just to be clear then you're inclined to  support a trade deal  we'll we'll see the detail when it comes  but you know our starting point is that  we want a deal  okay so that would definitely be our  starting point okay now pretty patel  has apologized after an official report  found she breached the  ministerial code and her behaviour  amounted to bullying  should she resign  i think her position is completely  untenable absolutely she's been found  uh by an independent inquiry of breaking  the ministerial code  uh and i think if you are found of  breaking the ministerial code  your position is is completely untenable  and i think it's appalling that the  prime minister  has decided to uh back her instead of  sacking her  and that's what he should have done and  just to follow up her on that  last year luciana berger left the labour  party because of what she described as a  culture of bullying bigotry  and intimidation tom watson said that  she was bullied out of her own party  by racist thugs so is labour only tough  on bullying when it's the conservatives  not at all i think look bullying uh  doesn't know  party political lines and i think we can  all be found  uh wanting when that comes and and  certainly the the behavior that luciana  was subject to  was appalling it was gross it was  grotesque  and that's why kierstarm has been very  clear that rooting out anti-semitism and  taking a tough line  on those who are anti-semitic or indeed  those who are  acting in a bullying way won't have a  place  in the labour party and he's very clear  that he would have he would have sacked  pretty patel or any other labour mp  who'd been  uh found wanting in that in that way so  it's it's not that  bullying can't exist in in all political  parties of course it can and it  does believe me it does it's how you  deal with it it's how you tackle it  and it's whether you show the leadership  and the tough decisions that you need to  take  to root it out talking about leadership  and tough decisions jeremy corbyn's been  suspended from  labour after his reaction to a report on  anti-semitism in the party  and just a very brief answer because we  are running out of time but  should jeremy corbyn be your labour mp  well look kier has taken uh the the  very clear decision to remove uh the  whip from  jeremy that's his uh right as leader  because  uh he was not happy uh with the response  that jeremy gave  in reaction to the very damning ehrc  report recently so i think as a very  first step  jeremy corbyn should apologize for for  that  uh reaction and hopefully then we can  all move on from this saga  okay lucy powell thank you very much for  being on the program thank you  very much for being here thank you
 

